==================================================================
DAY 29 - PART A: THEORY
Resource Requests, Limits, and QoS Classes
Time: 40 minutes
==================================================================

1. WHY THIS MATTERS
-------------------
Without resource settings, one buggy pod can eat all CPU or memory on
a node and hurt every other pod there. The scheduler also has no idea
how big a pod is, so it may pack too many onto one node.


2. REQUESTS AND LIMITS
----------------------
  resources:
    requests:          # what the pod is GUARANTEED / reserves
      cpu: "250m"
      memory: "128Mi"
    limits:            # the MAXIMUM it may use
      cpu: "500m"
      memory: "256Mi"

REQUESTS are used by the SCHEDULER: a node fits a pod only if
  sum of requests on node + this request <= node allocatable.
Requests are reservations, not usage: a node can be "full" with
requests while CPUs sit idle.

LIMITS are enforced by the KERNEL (cgroups, Day 2) at runtime.


3. UNITS
--------
CPU:     1 = one core (vCPU).  "500m" = 0.5 core (millicores).
Memory:  bytes. Use Mi/Gi (powers of 2): 128Mi, 1Gi.
         Careful: "128M" (powers of 10) and "128m" (= 0.128 bytes!)
         are different.


4. CPU vs MEMORY BEHAVE DIFFERENTLY
-----------------------------------
CPU is COMPRESSIBLE:
  over the limit -> THROTTLED (slowed down), never killed.
  cgroup cpu.max = quota per 100ms period.
MEMORY is NOT compressible:
  over the limit -> OOMKilled (exit 137), container restarted.

Many teams set a memory limit always, and CPU limit carefully (or not
at all) because throttling causes latency spikes even when the node
has idle CPU.


5. QoS CLASSES
--------------
Kubernetes assigns each pod a Quality of Service class:

  Guaranteed   every container has requests == limits for BOTH cpu
               and memory
  Burstable    at least one request or limit set, but not Guaranteed
  BestEffort   nothing set at all

When a NODE runs out of memory, the kubelet EVICTS pods in this order:
  BestEffort first -> Burstable (using most above request) ->
  Guaranteed last.

  k get pod X -o jsonpath='{.status.qosClass}'


6. NAMESPACE GUARDRAILS
-----------------------
LimitRange (per namespace):
  default requests/limits for containers that don't set them,
  plus min/max per container.
ResourceQuota (per namespace):
  total caps: requests.cpu, limits.memory, pods, services,
  persistentvolumeclaims, requests.storage ...
  Once a quota on cpu/memory exists, every new pod MUST specify
  them (or get defaults from a LimitRange), otherwise it's rejected.


7. MEASURING REAL USAGE
-----------------------
metrics-server collects CPU/memory from kubelets:
  k top nodes
  k top pods
Set requests near typical usage, memory limit above peak.


KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
----------------------
- Requests = scheduling; limits = enforcement.
- CPU over limit = throttled; memory over limit = OOMKilled.
- QoS decides who gets evicted first.
- LimitRange = defaults per container; ResourceQuota = namespace total.
